A checkpoint must not resurrect an older copy over a newer one

0041 routed checkpoints through the replay write batch, and the batch's
cache-sync came with it unexamined. jbd_replay_wb_sync_bcache() rewrites a
cache-resident copy of each written block, which is correct in the context
it was built for: during recovery the log is the newest truth and a stale
resident would later be flushed over the replayed data.

A checkpoint reverses that ranking. When the queue holds two transactions
that both journaled the same block -- the shape of every hot metadata
block, an inode table block above all -- the older transaction's flush
reads its own logged copy (the resident belongs to the newer one, so the
collect loop rightly takes the from-the-log branch) and then synced those
stale bytes over the newer transaction's dirty resident, clearing
BC_DIRTY as it went. The newer flush then recorded the clobbered buffer
as if it were current. The newest version survived only in log blocks
nothing would read again, and the block landed on disk one transaction
stale under a green unmount.

Found on a mounted volume, not by a suite: a cleanly unmounted LUKS2
filesystem where e2fsck counted one more subdirectory than the root
inode's link count -- the final mkdir's increment, checkpointed away. The
same mechanism corrupts what LATER commits journal (they read the
clobbered cache), which is how a synced operation could vanish across a
crash-and-replay even though its commit reached the log: the mount-crash
suite's intermittent "durable once synced" failures fit exactly this.

The fix is one rule: a checkpoint's cache-sync leaves dirty residents
alone. Dirty means a newer transaction's flush is still coming, and that
flush writes the block home after ours; clean residents still take the
sync, which is what keeps the cache coherent with the direct write.
Recovery keeps today's behavior via a mode flag on the batch.

Red first: four mkdirs, one transaction each (EXT4B_TXN_BATCH=1), clean
unmount -- e2fsck found /d's inode unallocated, both bitmaps stale, and
the group's directory count wrong. Green with this patch; the cell lives
in Tests/run_write_tests.sh ("a checkpointed hot block keeps its newest
copy").

